        "original_id": "867a427b-7758-5dcf-87c0-60ba503ad424",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: John Wesley, Charles Wesley, George Whitefield, Edward Perronet\n- Organizations: Church of England, Methodism, St Peter's, Eastern Hill\n- Places: England, Melbourne, Victoria, Australia\n- Roles: Anglican cleric, theologian, Anglican priest, parish church\n- Concepts: 18th-century revival\n\nFacts:\n- John Wesley was born on 28 June 1703 and died on 2 March 1791.\n- Edward Perronet was born in 1726 and died on 2 January 1792.\n- Methodism originated as a revival within the 18th-century Church of England.\n- John Wesley was an English Anglican cleric and theologian.\n- John Wesley, Charles Wesley, and George Whitefield founded Methodism.\n- Edward Perronet was the son of an Anglican priest.\n- Edward Perronet worked closely with Anglican priest John Wesley and his brother Charles Wesley for many years in England's eighteenth-century revival.\n- Methodism became a separate denomination after John Wesley's death.\n- St Peter's, Eastern Hill is the Anglican parish church of the City of Melbourne, Victoria, Australia.",
